---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Orforglipron (Foundayo): Approved Use — Nawtch"
  description="Foundayo (orforglipron), FDA-approved April 2026 — the first oral non-peptide GLP-1 pill for weight management. Approved use and dosing info."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/peptides/orforglipron/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
      <header>
        <div class="header-left">
          <h1>Orforglipron (Foundayo)</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">The first oral non-peptide GLP-1 — FDA-approved for chronic weight management.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/peptides/">← Back to Peptides</a>
      </header>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent); background:rgba(200,240,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Last Verified: August 14, 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent2); background:rgba(96,200,240,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent2);">Tier 1 — FDA-Approved Prescription Medication</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Orforglipron is a real, regulated drug with clinical trial data behind its approved
            use, available only by prescription. This page describes its approved use and
            standard prescribing information — that's public medical information about a
            regulated drug, not personalized medical advice. <strong>Talk to your doctor</strong> about whether it's appropriate for you.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Classification Note</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Strictly speaking, <strong>orforglipron is not a peptide</strong> — it's a small
            molecule taken by mouth, chemically unrelated to the injectable GLP-1 peptides like
            semaglutide and tirzepatide elsewhere in this tier. We include it here because it
            works through the same GLP-1 receptor mechanism and belongs in the same
            approved-medication conversation, not because it's structurally a peptide.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Orforglipron is a non-peptide small-molecule GLP-1 receptor agonist — it activates the
            same receptor as injectable GLP-1 drugs, stimulating glucose-dependent insulin
            release, suppressing glucagon, slowing gastric emptying, and acting on appetite
            centers in the brain. Because it's a small molecule rather than a peptide, it survives
            digestion well enough to be taken as a standard pill, with no food or water timing
            restrictions — unlike earlier oral GLP-1 tablets that required specific fasting
            conditions to absorb properly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">FDA-Approved Use</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            The FDA approved <strong>Foundayo</strong> (orforglipron) on April 1, 2026, for chronic
            weight management in adults with obesity, or overweight with at least one
            weight-related condition, alongside diet and exercise. Approval was supported by the
            ATTAIN-1 Phase 3 trial, in which adults on the highest studied dose lost an average of
            roughly 27 pounds. It is currently approved for weight management only — not for type
            2 diabetes, though Eli Lilly has diabetes trials underway.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://investor.lilly.com/news-releases/news-release-details/fda-approves-lillys-foundayotm-orforglipron-only-glp-1-pill" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eli Lilly — FDA Approval Announcement →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Standard Prescribing Information</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Like injectable GLP-1 drugs, orforglipron is titrated up gradually to reduce GI side
            effects, starting at a low non-therapeutic dose and stepping up over several weeks to
            a maintenance dose set by a prescriber. It's taken once daily as a tablet, with no
            requirement to take it on an empty stomach or wait to eat or drink afterward — a
            practical difference from Rybelsus (oral semaglutide). This is general public
            prescribing information, not a personalized recommendation — your prescriber sets your
            actual dose and schedule.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Boxed Warning &amp; Who Should Be Cautious</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            As a GLP-1 receptor agonist, orforglipron carries the same class boxed warning for
            thyroid C-cell tumors observed in rodent studies (human relevance not established);
            it's contraindicated for anyone with a personal or family history of medullary
            thyroid carcinoma or Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia syndrome type 2. Other risks include
            pancreatitis, gallbladder disease, and hypoglycemia when combined with insulin or
            sulfonylureas. The most common side effects are gastrointestinal — nausea, diarrhea,
            vomiting, and constipation — especially during dose increases.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <a href="https://www.ajmc.com/view/fda-approves-lilly-s-oral-glp-1-orforglipron-for-obesity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AJMC — FDA Approves Lilly's Oral GLP-1 Orforglipron for Obesity</a>
        </div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
